      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find \\( x^2 + y^2 + z^2 \\) given that the matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) satisfies \\(\\mathbf{M}^T \\mathbf{M} = \\mathbf{I}\\).\n\nFirst, let's compute \\(\\mathbf{M}^T \\mathbf{M}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} \n0 & 2y & z \\\\ \nx & y & -z \\\\ \nx & -y & z \n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThe transpose of \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M}^T = \\begin{pmatrix} \n0 & x & x \\\\ \n2y & y & -y \\\\ \nz & -z & z \n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nNow we need to calculate the product \\(\\mathbf{M}^T \\mathbf{M}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M}^T \\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} \n0 & x & x \\\\ \n2y & y & -y \\\\ \nz & -z & z \n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\begin{pmatrix} \n0 & 2y & z \\\\ \nx & y & -z \\\\ \nx & -y & z \n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nWe will compute each element of the resulting matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}^T \\mathbf{M}\\):\n\n1. First row, first column:\n\\[\n0 \\cdot 0 + x \\cdot x + x \\cdot x = x^2 + x^2 = 2x^2\n\\]\n\n2. First row, second column:\n\\[\n0 \\cdot 2y + x \\cdot y + x \\cdot (-y) = 0 + xy - xy = 0\n\\]\n\n3. First row, third column:\n\\[\n0 \\cdot z + x \\cdot (-z) + x \\cdot z = 0 - zx + zx = 0\n\\]\n\n4. Second row, first column:\n\\[\n2y \\cdot 0 + y \\cdot x + (-y) \\cdot x = 0 + xy - xy = 0\n\\]\n\n5. Second row, second column:\n\\[\n2y \\cdot 2y + y \\cdot y + (-y) \\cdot (-y) = 4y^2 + y^2 + y^2 = 6y^2\n\\]\n\n6. Second row, third column:\n\\[\n2y \\cdot z + y \\cdot (-z) + (-y) \\cdot z = 2yz - yz - yz = 0\n\\]\n\n7. Third row, first column:\n\\[\nz \\cdot 0 + (-z) \\cdot x + z \\cdot x = 0 - zx + zx = 0\n\\]\n\n8. Third row, second column:\n\\[\nz \\cdot 2y + (-z) \\cdot y + z \\cdot (-y) = 2yz - yz - yz = 0\n\\]\n\n9. Third row, third column:\n\\[\nz \\cdot z + (-z) \\cdot (-z) + z \\cdot z = z^2 + z^2 + z^2 = 3z^2\n\\]\n\nPutting it all together, we get:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M}^T \\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} \n2x^2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \n0 & 6y^2 & 0 \\\\ \n0 & 0 & 3z^2 \n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\mathbf{M}^T \\mathbf{M} = \\mathbf{I}\\), this implies:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} \n2x^2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \n0 & 6y^2 & 0 \\\\ \n0 & 0 & 3z^2 \n\\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \n1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \n0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \n0 & 0 & 1 \n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nFrom this, we can equate the corresponding elements:\n\\[\n2x^2 = 1 \\implies x^2 = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\n6y^2 = 1 \\implies y^2 = \\frac{1}{6}\n\\]\n\\[\n3z^2 = 1 \\implies z^2 = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nSumming these squares, we get:\n\\[\nx^2 + y^2 + z^2 = \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nTo add these fractions, we need a common denominator. The least common multiple of 2, 6, and 3 is 6:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{3}{6}, \\quad \\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{1}{6}, \\quad \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{2}{6}\n\\]\n\nAdding these fractions together:\n\\[\nx^2 + y^2 + z^2 = \\frac{3}{6} + \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{6}{6} = 1\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]",
