      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "From the problem statement, we have\n\\[\\mathbf{M}^T \\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & x & x \\\\ 2y & y & -y \\\\ z & -z & z \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 2y & z \\\\ x & y & -z \\\\ x & -y & z \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2x^2 & 0 & xz \\\\ 0 & 6y^2 & -xy \\\\ xz & -xy & z^2 + x^2 + y^2 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]Since $\\mathbf{M}^T \\mathbf{M} = \\mathbf{I},$ all of the entries in the resulting matrix must be 1.  Therefore,\n\\[2x^2 = 1,\\]which implies $x = \\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.$  Similarly, $y = \\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}},$ and $z = \\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.$  Thus,\n\\[x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = \\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)^2 = \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{2} = \\boxed{1}.\\]. The answer is 1",
