      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "\\[(\\mathbf{M}^T \\mathbf{M})_{11} = x^2 + y^2 + z^2.\\]Also,\n\\[(\\mathbf{M}^T \\mathbf{M})_{12} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & x & x \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 2y \\\\ y \\\\ -y \\end{pmatrix} = 0,\\]whereas\n\\[(\\mathbf{M}^T \\mathbf{M})_{13} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & x & x \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} z \\\\ -z \\\\ z \\end{pmatrix} = 0.\\]Finally,\n\\[(\\mathbf{M}^T \\mathbf{M})_{22} = x^2 + y^2 + z^2.\\]Also, $$(\\mathbf{M}^T \\mathbf{M})_{23} = \\begin{pmatrix} x & 2y & z \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} z \\\\ -z \\\\ z \\end{pmatrix} = x|z|^{2} + 2y(-z)|z| + z|z|^2.$$\n\nThis must equal 1, so $x|z|^{2} + 2y(-z)|z| + z|z|^2 = 1,$ or $$x|z|^{2} + 2(-y)|z| + |z|^2 = \\frac{1}{|z|^{2}}.$$The left side is increasing with $|z|,$ so $z = 0,$ whence using $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = x^2 + y^2,$ we get \\[x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = \\boxed{1}.\\]. The answer is 1",
